if you need some machine-specific settings which should not be upstreamed,
	eg diff user.email on a work computer,
then rejoice, as you don't need to fully dup your defaults.

we can create a ~/.gitconfig which inherits the git config in your git-tracked dotfiles,
then overrides only certain values.

this is possible because...
1. when git is looking for a user's global git config, 
	it checks .gitconfig before checking ~/.config/git
2. git configs allow for `includes` statements
3. for config values which are set multiple times within a single config,
	the last declaration has highest precedence

so you can keep defaults (eg your upstreamable `user`) in dotfiles, linked to ~/.config/git,
then create a ~/.gitconfig with an include of ~/.config/git at the very top,

you can imagine that your effective config is just a `cat` 
	of the system git config in /etc,
	the user git config,
	the repo git config, 
in that order

and this strategy effectively augments that to being `cat` of
	of the system config in /etc,
	your ~/.config/git linked to this repo,
	your not-upstreamable ~/.gitconfig for the specific machine,
	the repo git config,
in that order

the only gotcha here is that any imperative 
`git config --global`
will add stuff to your `~/.gitconfig rather than your `~/.config/git/config`,
which you might not have meant to do and won't be easy to notice 
until it bites you when you expect the new config on another machine and it's missing.

my current workaround to this problem is a fish abbr that expands to --file ~/.config/git rather than --global
